<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Al-Ahsa Oasis</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as al-Hasa, Al-Ahsa, Al-Hasa</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Al-Ahsa is a large oasis in the Eastern Province of Saudi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>, about 60 kilometres inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>, and one of the largest date-palm oases in the world. Its towns, chief among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hofuf">Hofuf</a>, were held by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> from the mid-16th century, by the Banu Khalid from 1670 and by the first Saudi state from the 1790s; the Ottomans returned in 1871 until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a> took it in 1913. It is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/al-ahsa-oasis.jpg" alt="Al-Ahsa Oasis" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: കാക്കര · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jabal_Al_Qara_Cave_-_Al_Hassa,_Saudi_Arabia_%E0%B4%9C%E0%B4%AC%E0%B5%BD_%E0%B4%85%E0%B5%BD_%E0%B4%96%E0%B4%BE%E0%B4%B1_%E0%B4%97%E0%B5%81%E0%B4%B9,_%E0%B4%85%E0%B5%BD_%E0%B4%B9%E0%B4%B8,_%E0%B4%B8%E0%B5%97%E0%B4%A6%E0%B4%BF_%E0%B4%85%E0%B4%B1%E0%B5%87%E0%B4%AC%E0%B5%8D%E0%B4%AF_12.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Al-Ahsa, also known as al-Hasa or Hajar, is an oasis and historical region in eastern Saudi Arabia about 60 kilometres inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persian-gulf">Persian Gulf</a>. The name is the plural of al-Hisa, a landscape of accumulated sand over an impermeable layer, which holds rainwater as an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquifer">aquifer</a> that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a> can reach. Springs have drawn settlement and date-palm cultivation since prehistoric times. The name Hajar may be related to the ancient toponym Agarum, mentioned in Dilmunite inscriptions as the home of their chief deity <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/inzak">Inzak</a>, though this reading has been criticised and other sources place Agarum on the island of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/failaka-island">Failaka</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a> was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rashidun-caliphate">Rashidun Caliphate</a> in the 7th century and later passed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyads</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbasid-caliphate">Abbasids</a>. In 899 the Qarmatian leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abu-tahir-al-jannabi">Abu Tahir al-Jannabi</a> took control and declared the region independent of the Abbasid caliphate, with a capital at al-Mu'miniya near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hofuf">Hofuf</a>. The Uyunids overthrew the Qarmatian state in 1077, followed by the Usfurids and the Jabrids. In 1550 Al-Ahsa and nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a> came under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> under Suleiman I, usually as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> region, and the Ottomans were expelled in 1670, when the Banu Khalid Emirate took over.</p><p>Al-Ahsa was incorporated into the Wahhabist Emirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diriyah">Diriyah</a> in 1795, returned to Ottoman control in 1818 through an invasion ordered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-of-egypt">Muhammad Ali of Egypt</a>, and was retaken by the Emirate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/najd">Nejd</a> in 1830. Direct Ottoman rule was restored in 1871, first under Baghdad Vilayet and from 1875 under Basra Vilayet. In 1913 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-saud">Ibn Saud</a>, founder of modern Saudi Arabia, annexed Al-Ahsa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qatif">Qatif</a> to Najd. Petroleum deposits found near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dammam">Dammam</a> in 1938 brought rapid modernisation.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The oasis has four main cities and 22 villages, among them Al-Mubarraz and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hofuf">Al-Hofuf</a>, and more than 2.5 million palm trees, watered by an underground <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aquifer">aquifer</a> and over 280 artesian springs. The region is known for tailoring, especially the bisht cloak, and was one of the few parts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabian-peninsula">Arabian Peninsula</a> where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> was grown. It became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 2018 as the Cultural Landscape of Al-Ahsa Oasis, which comprises 12 locations, among them the Suq Al-Qaysariyah, the forts of Qasr Khuzam, Qasr Sahood and Qasr Ibrahim, and the Jawatha Mosque.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Al-Ahsa Oasis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>With the Ikhwan, a tribal army inspired by Wahhabism and led by Faisal Al-Dawish, he took <b>Al-Ahsa</b> from the Ottomans in 1913, stayed out of the Arab Revolt of 1916 that made Hussein bin Ali King of Hejaz, defeated the Al Rashid and took the title Sultan of…</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudi-arabia">Saudi Arabia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1670 their chief Barrak ibn Ghurayr expelled the Ottoman garrison of the Lahsa Eyalet and made Hofuf the centre of an emirate controlling the coast from Kuwait to Qatar and the oases of <b>al-Hasa</b> and Qatif.</blockquote><a href="/state/banu-khalid-emirate">Banu Khalid Emirate</a></li><li><blockquote>The emirate expanded by war and conversion, taking Riyadh in 1773, defeating the Banu Khalid Emirate and conquering <b>al-Hasa</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-diriyah">Emirate of Diriyah</a></li><li><blockquote>He restored Saudi rule over Najd and <b>al-Hasa</b> and renewed the alliance with the Wahhabi scholars.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-nejd">Emirate of Nejd</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1913 he took <b>al-Hasa</b> from the Ottoman Empire, gaining the Gulf coast.</blockquote><a href="/state/emirate-of-nejd-and-hasa">Emirate of Nejd and Hasa</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1440 they took <b>al-Ahsa</b> and then Qatif and the Bahrain islands from the Jarwanid dynasty, establishing a Sunni dynasty based in al-Ahsa.</blockquote><a href="/state/jabrid-dynasty">Jabrid dynasty</a></li></ul></section>
